# XSH ccosh(), ccoshf() and ccoshl()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Only what the page makes exact is tested: special values (signed zeros,
# infinities, NaN parts), the symmetry rules and the exact results it states.
# The accuracy of other results is not tested. Arguments are built with
# CMPLX() and pass through volatile objects, and the three types (name, name
# with f, name with l) are each tested. The special values on this page carry
# the tag for the IEC 60559 Complex Floating-Point option and are NOTINUSE
# where it is not claimed.

assertion  XSH.ccosh.001
anchor     functions/ccosh.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       For ccosh, w is the argument. w = +0 + i0 gives 1 + i0.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Complex Floating-Point
           option; the test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it
           (Annex G of the C standard). No error is defined on this page, so
           errno is not examined; an exception is checked only when
           math_errhandling includes MATH_ERREXCEPT.
test       t/001-zero.c

assertion  XSH.ccosh.002
anchor     functions/ccosh.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       For ccosh, w is the argument. w = +0 + iInf gives a NaN real part
           and a zero imaginary part of either sign, and the invalid exception
           is raised.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Complex Floating-Point
           option; the test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it
           (Annex G of the C standard). No error is defined on this page, so
           errno is not examined; an exception is checked only when
           math_errhandling includes MATH_ERREXCEPT.
test       t/002-zero-inf.c

assertion  XSH.ccosh.003
anchor     functions/ccosh.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       For ccosh, w is the argument. w = +0 + iNaN gives a NaN real part
           and a zero imaginary part of either sign.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Complex Floating-Point
           option; the test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it
           (Annex G of the C standard). No error is defined on this page, so
           errno is not examined; an exception is checked only when
           math_errhandling includes MATH_ERREXCEPT.
test       t/003-zero-nan.c

assertion  XSH.ccosh.004
anchor     functions/ccosh.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       For ccosh, w is the argument. w = x + iInf (x non-zero finite)
           gives NaN + iNaN and the invalid exception is raised.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Complex Floating-Point
           option; the test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it
           (Annex G of the C standard). No error is defined on this page, so
           errno is not examined; an exception is checked only when
           math_errhandling includes MATH_ERREXCEPT.
test       t/004-nonzero-inf.c

assertion  XSH.ccosh.005
anchor     functions/ccosh.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       For ccosh, w is the argument. w = x + iNaN (x non-zero finite)
           gives NaN + iNaN.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Complex Floating-Point
           option; the test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it
           (Annex G of the C standard). No error is defined on this page, so
           errno is not examined; an exception is checked only when
           math_errhandling includes MATH_ERREXCEPT.
test       t/005-nonzero-nan.c

assertion  XSH.ccosh.006
anchor     functions/ccosh.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       For ccosh, w is the argument. w = +Inf + i0 gives +Inf + i0.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Complex Floating-Point
           option; the test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it
           (Annex G of the C standard). No error is defined on this page, so
           errno is not examined; an exception is checked only when
           math_errhandling includes MATH_ERREXCEPT.
test       t/006-plus-inf-zero.c

assertion  XSH.ccosh.007
anchor     functions/ccosh.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       For ccosh, w is the argument. w = +Inf + iy (y non-zero finite)
           gives positive infinity times cos(y) + i sin(y): each part is an
           infinity whose sign is that of the cosine or sine (y = 1, 2, 4, 5).
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Complex Floating-Point
           option; the test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it
           (Annex G of the C standard). No error is defined on this page, so
           errno is not examined; an exception is checked only when
           math_errhandling includes MATH_ERREXCEPT.
test       t/007-plus-inf-finite.c

assertion  XSH.ccosh.008
anchor     functions/ccosh.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       For ccosh, w is the argument. w = +Inf + iInf gives an infinite
           real part of either sign and a NaN imaginary part, and the invalid
           exception is raised.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Complex Floating-Point
           option; the test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it
           (Annex G of the C standard). No error is defined on this page, so
           errno is not examined; an exception is checked only when
           math_errhandling includes MATH_ERREXCEPT.
test       t/008-plus-inf-inf.c

assertion  XSH.ccosh.009
anchor     functions/ccosh.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       For ccosh, w is the argument. w = +Inf + iNaN gives +Inf + iNaN.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Complex Floating-Point
           option; the test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it
           (Annex G of the C standard). No error is defined on this page, so
           errno is not examined; an exception is checked only when
           math_errhandling includes MATH_ERREXCEPT.
test       t/009-plus-inf-nan.c

assertion  XSH.ccosh.010
anchor     functions/ccosh.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       For ccosh, w is the argument. w = NaN + i0 gives a NaN real part
           and a zero imaginary part of either sign.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Complex Floating-Point
           option; the test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it
           (Annex G of the C standard). No error is defined on this page, so
           errno is not examined; an exception is checked only when
           math_errhandling includes MATH_ERREXCEPT.
test       t/010-nan-zero.c

assertion  XSH.ccosh.011
anchor     functions/ccosh.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       For ccosh, w is the argument. w = NaN + iy (y non-zero finite) and
           w = NaN + iNaN give NaN + iNaN.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Complex Floating-Point
           option; the test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it
           (Annex G of the C standard). No error is defined on this page, so
           errno is not examined; an exception is checked only when
           math_errhandling includes MATH_ERREXCEPT.
test       t/011-nan-nonzero.c

assertion  XSH.ccosh.012
anchor     functions/ccosh.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       ccosh of the conjugate of z returns the conjugate of ccosh of z,
           part for part (equal values, zero signs included, NaN matching
           NaN), at ordinary points, at real-axis points where branch cuts
           lie, and at points with infinite parts and with a NaN real part.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Complex Floating-Point
           option; the test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it
           (Annex G of the C standard). No error is defined on this page, so
           errno is not examined; an exception is checked only when
           math_errhandling includes MATH_ERREXCEPT. Points with a NaN
           imaginary part are left out (and, for the odd and even rules,
           points with any NaN part): the sign of a NaN carries no required
           meaning. See Q-113.
test       t/012-conjugate-symmetry.c

assertion  XSH.ccosh.013
anchor     functions/ccosh.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       ccosh of -z returns the same value as ccosh of z, part for part
           (equal values, zero signs included), at ordinary points, at
           real-axis points where branch cuts lie, and at points with infinite
           parts.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Complex Floating-Point
           option; the test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it
           (Annex G of the C standard). No error is defined on this page, so
           errno is not examined; an exception is checked only when
           math_errhandling includes MATH_ERREXCEPT. Points with a NaN
           imaginary part are left out (and, for the odd and even rules,
           points with any NaN part): the sign of a NaN carries no required
           meaning. See Q-113.
test       t/013-even-symmetry.c
